# net: the network for programs, served by netstack (user/services/netstack)
# on /svc/net. A program whose list asks for it (`svc net`) finds it there;
# init publishes it and keeps the shared channel's server end across
# netstack's restarts. Each opener (libos's svc_open, svc_get) gets a
# channel of its own with the svc protocol's connect (abi/idl/svc.idl): a
# reply that comes later (an echo, a wait) goes to that caller's channel
# only. The shared channel itself answers connect, `iface` and `counts`.
# The same protocol is served on a second shared channel, /svc/net-sys,
# which init gives only the network's own services (dns, netlog, update;
# never a program from /data): its openers are SYSTEM openers, which may
# use a reserve of netstack's openers, sockets, waits and ring bytes that
# ordinary programs' openers can't (<net.h> NET_PROG_*). And on two more,
# the listen permissions: /svc/net-listen, whose openers may take the
# ports servers are known by (1024 to 49151) and listen for TCP, and
# /svc/net-low, whose openers may also use ports below 1024 (the
# shell gives it only to a program whose list says `svc net listen low`:
# bin/serve; never a program from /data).
# <net.h> has the client side (blocking helpers for programs, forms that
# don't wait for services' loops) and the limits; docs/HISTORY.md, M9
# and M9.5 have the design.
#
# Addresses are IPv4 addresses as numbers, the first byte highest
# (10.2.21.5 is 0x0a021505); ports are numbers. A datagram is at most
# NET_DGRAM_MAX (1472) bytes: one Ethernet frame, never fragmented.
#
# A protocol for each kind of channel (the file holds four): `net` is the
# opener's channel (and the shared channels', which answer connect, iface
# and counts); a socket's channel speaks net_socket (a UDP socket, and
# netctl's DHCP socket), a TCP connection's net_stream, a listener's
# net_listener. A method sent to another kind of channel is
# ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED, from the generated server: no kind has the others'
# methods. The opener's methods are iface, wait_change, counts,
# chip_counts, udp, udp_rings, echo, tcp and tcp_listener; at most
# NET_OPENERS openers (connect: ERR_NO_RESOURCES; NET_PROG_OPENERS of them
# ordinary ones). Closing an opener's channel closes its sockets and
# listeners too; closing a socket's, a connection's or a listener's closes
# it.
#
# A socket's datagrams travel through its rings (<sockring.h>: one VMO
# with a tx and an rx ring, and two events), never in a call: udp_rings
# makes a socket with its rings, net_socket.rings gives a socket its rings.
#
# Loopback: 127.0.0.0/8 is this host. A datagram or a TCP connection to an
# address in it goes to a socket of this same netstack, never to the network
# card, whatever the card's address and link are (netstack's stack.h has
# the how). Every address of the block is the one loopback interface,
# 127.0.0.1: a program that sends to 127.0.0.5 is answered from 127.0.0.1.
# The listen permissions are the same as anywhere. A packet with a loopback
# address in it is never sent to the network, and one that arrives from it
# is dropped.
#
# Limits, so one program can't use netstack up (<net.h>):
# NET_SOCKETS_PER_OPENER (256) sockets an opener, NET_SOCKETS_MAX (512) in
# all, NET_PROG_SOCKETS (384) of them ordinary openers'; NET_LATER_PER_OPENER
# (8) waits, echoes and chip_counts in flight an opener; ring bytes
# (sockring_bytes) SOCKRING_OPENER_BYTES an opener, SOCKRING_TOTAL_BYTES in
# all, NET_PROG_RING_BYTES of them ordinary openers'. A datagram that
# doesn't fit a socket's rx ring is dropped and counted (netstack never
# waits for a reader). TCP: NET_TCP_PER_OPENER connections an opener,
# NET_TCP_MAX in all, NET_PROG_TCP of them ordinary openers' (a listener's
# connections count against its opener from their handshake on);
# NET_LISTENERS_PER_OPENER listeners an opener, NET_LISTENERS_MAX in all,
# NET_PROG_LISTENERS ordinary openers'; their backlogs NET_BACKLOG_TOTAL
# together, NET_PROG_BACKLOG ordinary openers'. A TCP connection's window
# is its rx ring's free room: a reader that doesn't keep up stops its
# sender, never netstack.
#
# Reconnect: when netstack ends, every opener's and socket's channel sees
# ERR_PEER_CLOSED; open /svc/net again (svc_get does), whose connect waits
# (bounded by the caller's deadline) on the shared channel, which init
# keeps, for the restarted netstack; then open the sockets again. A new
# netstack starts with no address until init or the DHCP client sets it
# (wait_change).
# Format: see tools/genidl.py.

protocol net 29

# The rule for time in this protocol (the M12 plan's Q19): a call has its
# caller's deadline, and a method carries a `timeout_ms` only where the
# server bounds a wait the caller's deadline cannot (it holds a slot of its
# own for the wait: an echo in flight, a change awaited, a connection
# awaited). Every such timeout is 1 to TIMEOUT_MAX milliseconds; where a
# wait may be endless it can also be WAIT_FOREVER (wait_change, accept); 0
# is accepted only where it means "don't wait" (accept). Anything else is
# ERR_INVALID_ARGS, and the call is refused before it holds anything.
const WAIT_FOREVER  0xffffffff
const TIMEOUT_MAX   60000

# On an opener's channel (or the shared one): the interface. Its address,
# mask and gateway (0: none), the DNS servers (0: none), its MAC address;
# `device` 1 while a network card's driver has a session with netstack,
# `link` 1 while its link is up; the VLAN every frame is tagged with (0
# without a card; 0x1000, NET_VLAN_UNTAGGED, on an untagged network), the
# link speed in Mb/s (0 while down); `version` counts
# the changes to the address, gateway and DNS servers since netstack
# started (1 at the start): pass it to wait_change.
1  iface  ()  -> (u32 address, u32 mask, u32 gateway, u32 dns1, u32 dns2, u8[6] mac, u8 device, u8 link, u16 vlan, u32 speed, u32 version)

# On an opener's channel: answered when iface's `version` is no longer
# `version` (at once if it isn't), with the new one; ERR_TIMED_OUT after
# timeout_ms (1..TIMEOUT_MAX, or WAIT_FOREVER: no timeout; else
# ERR_INVALID_ARGS). How a program waits for an address (<net.h>
# net_wait_up) or for new DNS servers.
2  wait_change  (u32 version, u32 timeout_ms)  -> (u32 version) later

# On an opener's channel (or the shared one): netstack's counts, as
# <net.h>'s struct net_counters (little-endian, its reserved fields 0).
# `counts` is that struct's bytes, at most 256: a caller given fewer (a
# netstack built before the struct grew) reads the rest as zeros, and one
# given more than its struct knows ignores them.
3  counts  ()  -> (u8[<=256] counts)

# On an opener's channel: the network card's own counts, as the driver
# answers netdev.stats (<jam/netdev.h> struct netdev_stats). ERR_NOT_FOUND:
# no session with a card's driver; ERR_PEER_CLOSED: the session ended
# before the driver answered. `counts` is as netdev.stats': that struct's
# bytes, at most 256, the rest read as zeros.
4  chip_counts  ()  -> (u8[<=256] counts) later

# On an opener's channel: a UDP socket on local port `port`, any local
# address, without its rings yet (net_socket.rings gives them). port 0: netstack
# picks one (49152 and up). Results: `socket`, a channel of its own that
# speaks net_socket (closing it closes the socket), and its port.
# ERR_ACCESS_DENIED: a port below 1024 on an opener that didn't come
# through /svc/net-low (and 67 or 68 on any: netctl's DHCP socket
# has one), or one below 49152 on an opener that came through neither it
# nor /svc/net-listen (the listen permissions, netstack's listen.h);
# ERR_ALREADY_BOUND: the port is taken; ERR_NO_RESOURCES: the opener has
# NET_SOCKETS_PER_OPENER sockets, NET_SOCKETS_MAX are open, or an
# ordinary opener's NET_PROG_SOCKETS are.
5  udp  (u16 port)  -> (handle socket, u16 port)

# On an opener's channel: an ICMP echo request (a ping) to `address` with
# `size` bytes of data (0..NET_DGRAM_MAX) and sequence number `seq`, from
# the opener's own echo id (netstack picks it: no opener sees another's
# replies). Answered when the reply comes: the round trip in microseconds,
# the reply's TTL and its data's size; ERR_TIMED_OUT after timeout_ms
# (1..TIMEOUT_MAX); ERR_NOT_FOUND: a router or the host said it is unreachable
# (ICMP destination unreachable). At once: ERR_INVALID_ARGS (not a unicast
# or loopback address, a size out of range, a timeout_ms that isn't 1..TIMEOUT_MAX), ERR_BAD_STATE (no address, the
# link down, no route; or an echo with this seq to this address is in
# flight), ERR_NO_RESOURCES (NET_LATER_PER_OPENER in flight already).
6  echo  (u32 address, u16 seq, u16 size, u32 timeout_ms)  -> (u32 rtt_us, u8 ttl, u16 size) later

# On an opener's channel: udp(port) and the socket's net_socket.rings(
# tx_bytes, rx_bytes) in one call: the socket's channel, its rings' VMO and events
# (<sockring.h>: SOCKRING_VMO_RIGHTS, SOCKRING_TO_STACK_RIGHTS,
# SOCKRING_TO_PROG_RIGHTS; datagram framing), its port and the rings'
# sizes. The errors of both.
7  udp_rings  (u16 port, u32 tx_bytes, u32 rx_bytes)  -> (handle socket, handle ring, handle to_stack, handle to_prog, u16 port, u32 tx_bytes, u32 rx_bytes)

# On an opener's channel: a TCP connection to address:port from a port
# netstack picks, with its rings (<sockring.h>, byte-stream framing;
# tx_bytes and rx_bytes 0 for NET_TCP_TX and NET_TCP_RX, else a ring size):
# its socket's channel (net_stream), the rings' VMO and events (as
# udp_rings'), its local port and the rings' sizes. Answered at once, CONNECTING: the
# status line says when it is OPEN, or CLOSED and why (SOCKRING_SIG_STATE):
# ERR_NOT_FOUND refused (a reset for our SYN), ERR_TIMED_OUT no answer or
# the peer stopped answering, ERR_PEER_CLOSED reset by the peer,
# ERR_BAD_STATE our address went away, ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE a count the program
# wrote in its rings was out of range (the connection is reset); OK when
# both directions ended and our FIN was acked. The bytes go only through
# the rings: SOCKRING_END on the tx ring is a FIN after the last byte (the
# program shut its sending down), on the rx ring the peer's FIN. Closing
# the channel closes the connection: with a FIN after what netstack took
# from the tx ring, or a reset if bytes the program never read are left
# in the rx ring. At once: ERR_INVALID_ARGS (an address a program can't
# send to, port 0, a ring size not allowed); ERR_BAD_STATE (no address, no
# route or the link down); ERR_NO_RESOURCES (the opener's, all openers' or
# an ordinary opener's share of connections or ring bytes; no local port).
8  tcp  (u32 address, u16 port, u32 tx_bytes, u32 rx_bytes)  -> (handle socket, handle ring, handle to_stack, handle to_prog, u16 port, u32 tx_bytes, u32 rx_bytes)

# On an opener's channel that came through /svc/net-listen or
# /svc/net-low (the listen permissions, netstack's listen.h;
# ERR_ACCESS_DENIED on any other): listen on TCP port `port` (NET_PORT_LOW
# and up; through /svc/net-low from 1, but not 67 or 68; 0:
# netstack picks one, NET_PORT_EPHEMERAL and up) for at most `backlog`
# (1..NET_BACKLOG_MAX) connections half-open or waiting for accept. Each
# gets rings of tx_bytes and rx_bytes (0: NET_TCP_TX, NET_TCP_RX) when its
# handshake finishes, so bytes that come before accept wait in them; one
# whose tx ring doesn't fit its opener's ring bytes then gets NET_TCP_TX,
# and one that doesn't fit the limits even so is reset. Results: the
# listener's channel (net_listener: accept; closing it stops listening and
# resets the connections waiting) and its port. ERR_ACCESS_DENIED also for a port
# below NET_PORT_LOW on a /svc/net-listen opener; ERR_ALREADY_BOUND: the
# port is taken; ERR_INVALID_ARGS: a backlog or ring size not allowed;
# ERR_NO_RESOURCES: the opener's, all openers' or an ordinary opener's
# share of listeners or of backlog.
#
# `flags` is 0, LISTEN_REUSE, LISTEN_LOOPBACK or both (any other bit:
# ERR_INVALID_ARGS). LISTEN_LOOPBACK listens on 127.0.0.1 only (POSIX's bind
# to a loopback address): a connection to a loopback address reaches the
# listener, one to the card's address doesn't. Without it the listener is on
# every address, loopback included.
#
# LISTEN_REUSE is POSIX's SO_REUSEADDR, as Linux does it. Without it the port must be free of every
# connection too: one still in TIME_WAIT, or open, from an earlier listener
# on the port is ERR_ALREADY_BOUND, so a server restarted at once can't
# listen. With it a listener can be made on a port whose old connections
# are in TIME_WAIT, and on one whose open connections came from a listener
# that was made with it as well; an active listener on the port still
# makes ERR_ALREADY_BOUND, with or without it (the flag doesn't share a
# port). A listener's connections keep its flag: they are what a later
# listener with the flag may stand beside.
const LISTEN_REUSE    1
const LISTEN_LOOPBACK 2
9  tcp_listener  (u16 port, u32 backlog, u32 tx_bytes, u32 rx_bytes, u32 flags)  -> (handle listener, u16 port)

# net_socket: a UDP socket's channel, from net.udp or net.udp_rings (and
# netctl's dhcp_open, the DHCP socket). Closing it closes the socket.
#
# Reconnect: as net's: when netstack ends, the channel sees ERR_PEER_CLOSED
# and the socket is gone; open the socket again from a new opener.

protocol net_socket 60

# From now on only datagrams from address:port go into its rx ring (others
# are dropped, not counted as dropped), and a tx record to 0:0 goes there.
# Address 0 and port 0: any sender again. Datagrams in the ring already
# stay. ERR_INVALID_ARGS: an address a program can't send to;
# ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED on the DHCP socket.
1  connect  (u32 address, u16 port)  -> ()
# Its local address and port, the peer `connect` set (0: none), bytes
# waiting in its rx ring now, and datagrams dropped since it opened (its rx
# ring was full; also the status line's rx_dropped). The address is the
# interface's now (0: it has none): a socket is bound to every address, and
# this is the one its datagrams leave from.
2  state  ()  -> (u32 address, u16 port, u32 peer, u16 peer_port, u32 queued, u32 dropped)
# The socket's rings (<sockring.h>, datagram framing): their VMO and events
# with the rights the header names, and the rings' sizes. tx_bytes and
# rx_bytes: 0 for SOCKRING_UDP_TX and SOCKRING_UDP_RX, else a ring size
# (sockring_size_ok). After it the socket's datagrams go only through the
# rings: a tx record netstack refuses (an address a program can't send to,
# port 0, no route) counts in the status line's tx_refused, with its reason
# in `error`, and a datagram that doesn't fit the rx ring in rx_dropped.
# Once a socket: ERR_BAD_STATE for a second. ERR_INVALID_ARGS: a size not
# allowed; ERR_NO_RESOURCES: over the opener's or all sockets' ring bytes;
# ERR_NO_MEMORY: no memory.
3  rings  (u32 tx_bytes, u32 rx_bytes)  -> (handle ring, handle to_stack, handle to_prog, u32 tx_bytes, u32 rx_bytes)

# Bind the socket to 127.0.0.1 instead of every address (POSIX's bind to a
# loopback address). From now on only a datagram sent to a loopback address
# reaches it (one to the card's address, or a broadcast, doesn't), and it
# may send only to loopback addresses: a tx record to another is refused
# like any other (the status line's tx_refused, `error` ERR_INVALID_ARGS).
# Its port stays. ERR_ALREADY_BOUND: another socket has that port on
# 127.0.0.1 or on every address; ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED: the DHCP socket.
4  loopback_only  ()  -> ()

# net_stream: a TCP connection's channel, from net.tcp or net_listener.accept.
# Closing it closes the connection: with a FIN after what netstack took from
# the tx ring, or a reset if bytes the program never read are left in the rx
# ring. The bytes go through its rings (<sockring.h>), never through calls.
#
# Reconnect: as net's: when netstack ends, the channel sees ERR_PEER_CLOSED
# and the connection is gone (its status line says CLOSED, ERR_PEER_CLOSED).

protocol net_stream 61

# Its local address and port (the address the connection runs on, fixed when
# it was made, which a closed connection keeps answering; the port netstack
# picked for a connection `tcp` made, or the listener's for one `accept`
# gave), the peer's address and port (for an accepted connection too), and
# the bytes waiting in its rx ring now. (A stream drops nothing, its window
# stops the sender: it has no count of dropped bytes.) POSIX's getsockname
# and getpeername are this.
# Same for a connection `tcp` made that is still CONNECTING: the address the
# route chose, the peer asked for.
1  state  ()  -> (u32 address, u16 port, u32 peer, u16 peer_port, u32 queued)

# Options of the connection (POSIX's TCP_NODELAY, SO_KEEPALIVE, TCP_KEEPIDLE,
# TCP_KEEPINTVL and TCP_KEEPCNT; libjam's net_tcp_set_options). Each call
# sets all of them at once, none is remembered: `flags` is the whole set of
# the on/off options (OPT_NODELAY, OPT_KEEPALIVE; any other bit:
# ERR_INVALID_ARGS), and the three keep-alive numbers apply when
# OPT_KEEPALIVE is set (a program keeps its own copy for getsockopt):
# keep_idle, the seconds an idle connection waits
# before its first probe; keep_intvl, the seconds between probes; keep_cnt,
# the probes unanswered before the connection is reset (CLOSED,
# ERR_TIMED_OUT). Each is 0 for the default (7200, 75 and 9, as Linux's) or
# 1..KEEP_MAX (keep_cnt 1..KEEP_CNT_MAX); else ERR_INVALID_ARGS, and nothing
# changes.
#
# A connection starts with OPT_NODELAY set and OPT_KEEPALIVE clear: bytes a
# program publishes in its tx ring leave at once, however small (the ring's
# publish is the push, and a program that wants Nagle's coalescing asks for
# it: a call with OPT_NODELAY clear holds a small segment back while an
# earlier one is unacked). A connection a listener accepted starts the same
# way. Answered at once; a connection that is CLOSED already takes the call
# and ignores it (OK).
const OPT_NODELAY    1
const OPT_KEEPALIVE  2
const KEEP_MAX       32767
const KEEP_CNT_MAX   127
2  set_options  (u32 flags, u32 keep_idle, u32 keep_intvl, u32 keep_cnt)  -> ()

# net_listener: a TCP listener's channel, from net.tcp_listener. Closing it
# stops listening and resets the connections waiting.
#
# Reconnect: as net's: when netstack ends, the channel sees ERR_PEER_CLOSED
# and the listener is gone; listen again from a new opener.

protocol net_listener 62

# The oldest connection waiting, now the caller's: net.tcp's results (its
# status OPEN, or CLOSED already if the peer closed it meanwhile; its rx ring
# holds what came; the socket speaks net_stream), and its peer's address and
# port. Answered when there is one: timeout_ms 0 at once (ERR_SHOULD_WAIT:
# none waiting), else ERR_TIMED_OUT after timeout_ms (1..NET_TIMEOUT_MAX, or
# NET_WAIT_FOREVER: never; else ERR_INVALID_ARGS). One at a time a listener: ERR_BAD_STATE while another waits. A loop
# or a wait set sends it without waiting (net_listener_accept_send) and
# watches the channel for SIG_READABLE: the answer is there.
1  accept  (u32 timeout_ms)  -> (handle socket, handle ring, handle to_stack, handle to_prog, u32 peer, u16 peer_port, u32 tx_bytes, u32 rx_bytes) later
